devfreq: raise the floor when work arrives, not after it has been seen

0016 reported any sampling window containing execution as fully busy, which
reaches the top of the range under sustained load but is retrospective: after
an idle period the first job runs at whatever rate the device was left at, and
the rate only rises once a poll has observed work that has already finished. A
workload submitting one inference every few hundred milliseconds gets the worst
of both - every job slow, every gap boosted.

Raise a PM QoS minimum frequency instead, from rknpu_power_get() - the power
acquisition that precedes the work, which is broader than job submission and
also covers the GEM, debugfs and cleanup callers - and drop it when the device
powers down. The floor is the highest rate the part's own OPP table offers, so
a variant capped at 500 MHz boosts to 500 rather than to a rate it does not
have.

What the floor guarantees is the constraint, not the rate. pm_qos_update_target()
discards what the devfreq notifier returns and qos_notifier_call() logs an
update_devfreq() failure and returns NOTIFY_OK, so a successful
dev_pm_qos_update_request() means the constraint is installed and nothing more.
A transition that fails afterwards is only logged, and because the QoS core
returns early when the value is unchanged, a later identical acquisition will
not retry it.

Re-acquisition is synchronous, so a job arriving after the floor was released
still runs at full rate rather than waiting for a poll to notice it. The
release point therefore only decides how long an idle-but-powered device sits
high, and it gates entirely after power_put_delay regardless.

The QoS update runs the governor, which takes the devfreq and DVFS locks, so
it is issued outside power_lock.

With the floor carrying the policy, the governor no longer needs to be told a
half-idle device is busy, so dvfs_demand_metric now defaults off and devfreq
sees what 0014 actually measures.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

